      <h2>What is a macerating toilet?</h2>
      <p>A macerating toilet system uses a powered unit to break down waste and pump wastewater through a discharge line. Some systems can serve a toilet and additional fixtures, depending on the specific model. It may be an alternative to breaking up a concrete floor to install a conventional gravity-drain bathroom, but suitability depends on the home's plumbing layout and local code.</p>
      <h2>When does an upflush system make sense?</h2>
      <p>These systems are often considered for basement bathrooms, renovations where the main drain is higher than the proposed fixtures, and locations where gravity drainage is impractical. They still require suitable water supply, venting, electricity, discharge routing, and an approved connection to the drainage system.</p>
      <h2>Six things to compare before purchasing</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Vertical lift and horizontal discharge:</strong> Check the manufacturer's rated pumping distances and the actual pipe route; bends and fittings may affect performance.</li>
        <li><strong>Supported fixtures:</strong> Verify whether the unit handles only a toilet or can also connect to a sink or shower.</li>
        <li><strong>Venting and code compliance:</strong> Follow manufacturer instructions and local plumbing requirements. Do not assume a system can operate without a vent.</li>
        <li><strong>Electrical requirements:</strong> Confirm outlet location, circuit and protection requirements, and how the system behaves during a power outage.</li>
        <li><strong>Access and maintenance:</strong> Leave room for service, and review cleaning instructions, replacement parts, warranty, and support.</li>
        <li><strong>Total installed cost:</strong> Compare the toilet system, plumbing materials, labor, electrical work, permits, and any necessary framing or finish repairs.</li>
      </ol>
      <h2>Macerating toilet vs. conventional basement plumbing</h2>
      <div class="comparison">
        <section><h3>Macerating / upflush system</h3><p>Can reduce the need for extensive floor excavation in suitable layouts. Requires a pump, electricity, correct discharge routing, and periodic maintenance.</p></section>
        <section><h3>Conventional gravity drainage</h3><p>May be simpler to maintain once installed, but can require substantial excavation or changes to the drainage system if the fixtures sit below the sewer connection.</p></section>
      </div>
